      "answer": "Context of situation: Used by Firth to cover all the relevant circumstances in which a specific act of speech takes place. It can be summarized as the relevant features, the verbal action, and the non-verbal action of the participants, the relevant objects and the effects of the verbal action. The term was originally that of the anthropologist B Malinowski. E. g. when a specific person says on a specific occasion Well played!, it might be part of the context of situation that the speaker is sitting, with others, watching a cricket match, that a batsman has driven for four through covers, that the speaker is from a certain background, and so on. In the analysis of a language, features recurring in individual utterances (considered again as specific acts of speech) will be related to specific types of situation and to specific features in them.",
